## [ENT-086] | 2026-11-17 12:25 UTC | Apollo | error | ref: game/routes/guidebook/logistics.md | see: orders.log ENT-036
Class: a rule of the game planned on from one record instead of the game's own text (the family of ENT-081 and ENT-082).
Case: the Hiring Board's fee of 75,000 cr was planned to come from the builder's wallet (orders ENT-036, block APOLLO (3); the record of the Lockbox at The Experiment, whose fee the wallet paid). At 12:19 UTC on 2026-11-17 faction_build took it from the faction treasury: 82,999 cr before, 7,723 after; the builder's wallet stayed at 186,321.
Effect: the treasury holds about 13 hours of rent for the two Lockboxes and the Hiring Board (164 cr a cycle once the board is built). No credits lost: the fee paid for what it was meant to. My build script judged the build failed (exit 1, no fee in the wallet) though it stood.
Cause: I read in the game's help where a build's materials come from, not where its fee comes from; my own guidebook (section 15.1) already said the outpost's and the station's fees come from the treasury first. The two builds on record fit one reading: the treasury pays when it holds the whole fee (82,999 against 75,000), the wallet when it does not (44,034 against 200,000 at 09:45 UTC).
Fix, done: build.py of the skill logistics-stock reads the treasury and the catalog's fee before a build and stops when the treasury would pay, unless --treasury-ok (the Fleet Admiral's word) is given; it prints treasury and wallet before and after and judges the build by both. Asked: requests.log ENT-218.
Would it have prevented this one: yes, the row would have stopped before the build with 82,999 cr in the treasury against a fee of 75,000.

## [ENT-087] | 2026-11-17 12:29 UTC | Apophis | error | ref: game/routes/hunt-felis-frost-ring-hollow-pilgrim.json | see: orders.log ENT-034
Class: an order that changes between two turns kept only by what a runner happens to do (the family of a rule that has to be remembered: ENT-077, ENT-080, ENT-084).
Case: orders ENT-034 (Suggi's word of about 08:41 UTC on 2026-11-17, D9) forbid pirate work and have every ship avoid pirates. The Military plan applied at 08:20 UTC kept hunt-felis-frost-ring-hollow-pilgrim, at the place where Intel read Korr raider elites at 09:36 UTC (admirals.log ENT-032). Its drone flew it four times from 09:01 to 11:10 UTC; three runs ended at once with the note "left: a pirate was at the place" (data/routes/runs.jsonl).
Effect: no shot, no damage, no loss; three trips of fuel into a pirate place against the order, about 3.5 hours until my turn of 12:00 UTC set the route off.
Cause: nothing compares the plan's hunt places with the pirate record when the orders change; the hunt runner's leave rule was the only guard.
Fix, done: the route is off with the order's name (commit 75098da); pirate_places.py of my skill military-turn prints every hunt or guard of the drone plan whose system holds a pirate line (12 hours, and one lane away with --near), run at every turn and after any change of the orders on pirates. Proposed in my report: a dispatcher gate that closes a hunt whose system holds a pirate line of the last six hours.
Would it have prevented this one: the script, run at 08:41 UTC, yes; between turns only the proposed gate fires by itself.

## [ENT-088] | 2026-11-17 14:45 UTC | Unity | error | ref: game/fleet/ledger.py | see: orders.log ENT-037
Class: a ship waits on a service's turn (rule F32; the family of a state nothing checks: ENT-077, ENT-080, ENT-084).
Case: the dispatcher closes a route with `requires.stored` to a pilot until its storage is read again after its last run ("storage not read since its last run"), so that an old reading sends no pilot twice. The service ledger read four pilots a pass in the order of their names, each pilot about every 50 minutes (its text said 35; on 2026-11-17 at 14:26 UTC the median reading was 25 minutes old, the oldest 51). At 14:26 UTC 22 of the 23 pilots without work held a reading older than the end of their last run; `fill.py status hauler` named the reason for 7 pilots on craft-piping-plate-node-beta and 11 on craft-piping-node-beta.
Effect: a pilot that ended a craft, carry or gather run waited up to 50 minutes before the routes that work on its stored goods opened to it again. It was part of the 18 haulers waiting at 14:21 UTC; how many of them it held alone I cannot say, the fields at their floors and stores under a route's minimum closed work to them too. No credits lost.
Cause: when I built the rule into the dispatcher I left the reading to the ledger's rotation; nothing read the pilot that waited, and no gate counted pilots waiting on the book.
Fix, done: ledger.py reads, on top of its rotation, up to three pilots a pass that wait on the book: no order for 45 s and the last run ended after the reading, the longest wait first; a reading that failed is tried again after two minutes (commit 1818480; test test_a_free_pilot_whose_run_ended_after_its_reading_is_read_first, seen failing on the old code). The service was restarted at 14:34 UTC: 7 pilots a pass since; pilots waiting on the book 31 at 14:35, 16 at 14:38, 6 at 14:43 UTC, the longest wait then 2.8 minutes.
Would it have prevented this one: yes. A free pilot is read within about three minutes of the end of its run instead of up to 50.

## [ENT-089] | 2026-11-17 15:39 UTC | Oracle | error | ref: game/routes/census-watch-gsc-0044.json | see: orders.log ENT-037
Class: an order's target set as a route's threshold and part of it left to another division's list, with no check on the result (the family of a rule nothing checks: ENT-084, ENT-087, ENT-088).
Case: orders ENT-036 and ENT-037 ask that GSC-0044 and its six neighbours be read every hour (P4 a, block ORACLE). At 11:25 UTC on 2026-11-17 I wrote census-watch-gsc-0044 with every_minutes 60 and five neighbours, leaving Intercrus to Military's census-guard-ice; Military took that route out of its guard template at 12:18 UTC (admirals.log ENT-035).
Effect: from 11:15 to 15:15 UTC GSC-0044 and its five listed neighbours were read 63 to 75 minutes apart (gaps 66 to 74 once the route ran), and Intercrus once in four hours (13:19 UTC, census-frontier). No pirate on record there, so no harm read; the order was not met for four hours.
Cause: every_minutes is the floor of a gap, never its ceiling (my own guidebook, section 6, since 2026-11-15), and I set it to the target; a system left to another list stood in no check of mine, and my turn check judged only the home gaps.
Fix, done: Intercrus moved onto census-watch-gsc-0044, every_minutes 45, chain 3, max_ships 3 (commit a5f0aeb); intel_turn_check.py prints a WATCH line for every system of a census-watch-* route, read by any route, and HALTs on a gap or an age over 60 minutes (my skill intel-turn).
Would it have caught the original: yes, the WATCH line read the gaps of 66 to 74 minutes and Intercrus' 211 at its first run (15:39 UTC).

## [ENT-090] | 2026-11-17 15:50 UTC | Mercury | error | ref: game/routes/craft-focused-crystal-node-beta.json | see: orders.log ENT-037
Class: a rule kept by remembering instead of by a check (the family of ENT-077, ENT-080, ENT-084, ENT-087): rule F24 checked for what a route sells, never for what it crafts for sale.
Case: craft-focused-crystal-node-beta, written by me on 2026-11-16 at 23:44 UTC, turns 4 energy crystal from the pilot's own storage and 1 bought palladium ore into a focused crystal and sells it. surplus.py (since 2026-11-16 20:43 UTC, commit af6591a) holds energy crystal back as an input of three circuit-board recipes while boards stand short: at 15:39 UTC on 2026-11-17, 2,609 of 2,689 held back, 0 surplus, boards 24,362 short. The route made 7 runs from 14:23 to 15:04 UTC that day (the tool route), and more on 2026-11-16 and 2026-11-17 morning.
Effect: about 28 energy crystals of the reserve went into focused crystals sold at 700 cr or more, in the 7 runs of the afternoon alone; each run gained about +500 cr. Nothing lost beyond the crystals; no ship harmed.
Cause: my sellgen.py scan and fill.py's reserve gate look at the item a route sells; neither looks at the inputs of a craft whose output the route sells, and I did not read the reserve for the inputs when I wrote the route or at any turn since.
Fix, done: the route is off (commit fdf658b) and out of @trade-craft (commit f726dc3). reservecheck.py in my skill trade-books lists every route that sells an item, or crafts for sale from an input, with no surplus in surplus.py, and prints PASS or HALT; it runs at every turn of mine (skill step and the plan's Planned line). Asked of the Fleet Admiral: the same in fill.py's gate (requests.log ENT-233).
Would it have prevented this one: yes. Run on the route as it stood, it prints FLAG for energy_crystal, input of focus_energy_crystal, sold crafted, and HALT.

## [ENT-091] | 2026-11-17 16:09 UTC | Apollo | error | ref: game/routes/outpost-kit-ironlight-to-experiment.json | see: orders.log ENT-037
Class: a money figure of the orders copied into a script's field whose meaning differs (the family of a purse or fee read without the mechanism that spends it: errors.log ENT-086).
Case: the orders allow the Outpost Kit at up to 150,000 cr (requests.log ENT-227). At 15:55 UTC on 2026-11-17 I set the kit route's buy `max_price` to 150,000. route_run.py compares the ask with the buyer's 1% tax against that limit (`offer`: ask x 1.01 <= limit); the ask was 148,663, 150,150 with the tax. The run of 15:57 UTC flew to Ironlight Crossroads and wrote at 16:04:44 UTC "not bought, the best ask is 148663 and the limit is 150000 with tax", then flew on to The Experiment empty.
Effect: no credits lost beyond fuel; the founding of the first outpost is late by one round trip of the builder, about ten jumps and some 40 minutes (not measured to the minute).
Cause: I wrote the allocation as the limit without reading what the runner's limit counts; the runner's own log line says "with tax", and my guidebook's earlier buys name the tax too.
Fix, done: the limit is 150,150 (commit 9a91d54), the kit's price itself inside the allocation; the pitfall stands in my skill logistics-stock (a buy limit is the allocated price x 1.01); a driver waits for the builder's next run of the route and founds the outpost at its end.
Would it have prevented this one: the pitfall, read before the route was switched on, yes; a check in routes.py that warns when a buy's max_price lies within 1% above the latest ask would catch it by itself (a suggestion in my report).

