## [ENT-098] | 2026-11-17 20:39 UTC | Apollo | error | ref: game/routes/guidebook/logistics.md | see: orders.log ENT-044
Class: an engine call's option names written from memory instead of copied from a script that flies it (the fleet-play pitfall "an option with a wrong name"; the family of a rule kept by remembering: ENT-089, ENT-093, ENT-095).
Case: on 2026-11-17 at 19:58 UTC I wrote a new script of my skill logistics-stock (outpost.py) for the outpost test of orders ENT-042, block APOLLO (2). Its flight called the goals navigate-to-system with `systemId` and go-to-poi with `poiId`; the engine asks `targetSystemId` and `targetPoiId`, as my own found.py passes them. At 20:20:51 UTC the test pilot, docked at The Experiment with 20 fuel cells bought for the test, got "options.targetSystemId: Required" and "options.targetPoiId: Required".
Effect: about two minutes lost; no credit and no ship harmed. Stopping the row by hand, a `pkill -f` whose pattern my own shell's command line matched killed that shell; the pilot stood released for about 15 seconds with the cells aboard before my hand took it again (20:21:53 UTC). The test flew again from 20:22 UTC with the names corrected.
Cause: I wrote the flight from memory of prepare-at-station (`systemId`, `poiId`) and did not copy it from found.py, which flew the same two goals at 16:35 UTC.
Fix, done: outpost.py takes the option names of found.py; my skill logistics-stock names the option names of the three goals in its pitfalls and says to stop a row by its PID, never by a `pkill -f` pattern.
Would it have prevented this one: yes for the names, had the pitfall been read; a check that fires by itself would be one shared flight helper for every script of my skill (found.py, outpost.py), so a name is written once: planned for my next turn.

